.TH ZEN 1 "2026-11-17" "zen 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zen \- a text editor with nothing in it but a cursor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zen
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.I command
.B | zen
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zen
is a terminal text editor that shows your text and a cursor and nothing else:
no status bar, no line numbers, no syntax highlighting.
.PP
There are no Ctrl or Alt shortcuts. A command is a sequence of ordinary keys
typed quickly, such as
.B ;w
to save. The keys appear as text while they are typed; when the sequence
completes they are removed and the command runs. Typed slower than the time
limit (500 ms by default), a sequence is plain text. A sequence is matched
wherever it occurs, including inside a word.
.PP
The bottom row is used only while a command asks for something or a warning
is shown, and is empty again on the next key.
.PP
Text piped into
.B zen
becomes the text being edited.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Use this configuration file instead of the default one.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the default configuration, as a starting point for your own.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help text.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH KEY SEQUENCES
These are the defaults. Every one can be changed or switched off in the
configuration file.
.TS
l l.
;w	save
;W	save under a new name
;q	quit (refuses if there are unsaved changes)
;Q	quit and throw away unsaved changes
;x	save and quit
;u  ;r	undo, redo
;f	find
;n  ;N	next, previous match
;h	find and replace (y yes, n skip, a all, q stop)
;g	go to a line number
;{  ;}	jump to the blank line above, below
;v	start selecting, then move; again to stop
;a	select all
;c  ;d  ;p	copy, cut, paste
;k	delete the current line
;y	duplicate the current line
;[  ;]	move the current line up, down
;j	re-wrap the current paragraph
;#	word and character count
;t	typewriter scrolling on or off
;o	focus dimming on or off
;;date	insert today's date
;;time	insert the current time
.TE
.PP
Arrow keys, Home, End, PageUp, PageDown, Ctrl+arrows (by word), Ctrl+Home and
Ctrl+End (start and end of the text), Shift+arrows (select), Backspace, Delete,
Alt+Backspace (delete a word), Esc (drop the selection) and the mouse work as
usual.
.SH CONFIGURATION
All settings, key sequences, text expansions and shell commands live in one
file. To create it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkdir \-p ~/.config/zen
zen \-\-print\-config > ~/.config/zen/config.toml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every setting is explained by a comment beside it. Lines you delete fall back
to their defaults. A mistake in the file is reported with its line number
when
.B zen
starts.
.PP
Font and font size are set in the terminal emulator, not here.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/zen/config.toml
Your configuration.
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zen/config.toml
if that variable is set.)
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/zen/positions
Where the cursor was in each file, so files reopen where you left them.
.RI ( $XDG_STATE_HOME/zen
if that variable is set.)
.TP
.IR . name .zen\-recover
Unsaved text, kept beside the file being edited and offered back after a
crash. Removed on save. For a text with no name yet it is
.IR ~/.local/state/zen/unnamed.zen\-recover .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B zen notes.txt
Edit, or start, notes.txt.
.TP
.B zen
Start an empty text; saving asks for a name.
.TP
.B git log | zen
Edit the output of a command.
.SH NOTES
Files must be UTF\-8 text; anything else is refused rather than risked.
Linux only.
